== Prefrontal Energy Failure as the Unifying Substrate of ADHD-Like Executive Dysfunction in ME/CFS
<sec:prefrontal-executive-adhd>

The prefrontal cortex (PFC) governs executive functions: working memory, impulse inhibition, emotional regulation, attentional control, planning, and the ability to pause and choose a response rather than react. It is the most metabolically expensive region in the human brain. Under ATP scarcity, it is the first brain system to degrade. This single fact unifies the scattered evidence across this document — PFC vulnerability (@sec:brain-energy-cross-disease), catecholamine inverted-U pharmacology (@ch:medication-response-reference), energy triage hierarchy (@fig:energy-triage-hierarchy), same-root ADHD hypothesis (@spec:adhd-mecfs-same-root), and epidemiological ADHD-ME/CFS overlap — into one mechanistic chain: mitochondrial energy failure → selective prefrontal ATP deficit → executive dysfunction → ADHD-like clinical phenotype.

=== The PFC as the Brain's Metabolic Bottleneck

#key-point(title: [The PFC is the brain's most energy-expensive circuit])[
The PFC consumes disproportionately more glucose and oxygen per gram of tissue than any other cortical region. Persistent-firing mechanisms underlying working memory — sustained neural activity without external input — require continuous Na#super[+]/K#super[+]-ATPase pump operation, making them exquisitely sensitive to ATP availability @Constantinidis2018working. Functional neuroimaging consistently shows that cognitive tasks engaging the PFC produce the largest metabolic signal in the brain. This is not a design flaw — it is the price of higher cognition. The consequence: any condition that reduces cerebral ATP delivery or production will impair PFC function before any other brain function, and will impair executive function before memory, motor skills, or vital functions.
] <kp:pfc-metabolic-bottleneck>

The energy triage hierarchy (@fig:energy-triage-hierarchy) formalizes this: Tier 5 (executive function) requires 85% of normal energy budget to operate, Tier 6 (complex cognition) requires 95%, while Tier 1 (brainstem vital functions) requires only 30%. In ME/CFS, available CNS energy falls below the Tier 5 threshold but above the Tier 1–3 thresholds. The clinical result is exactly what patients report: preserved vital functions and basic sensory-motor processing, but impaired planning, impulse control, emotional regulation, sustained attention, and task-switching — the core ADHD symptom cluster.

=== Evidence Convegence: Five Independent Lines

*Line 1 — Cerebrovascular demand failure.* Ninety-one percent of ME/CFS patients with normal resting cerebral blood flow show abnormal CBF reduction during orthostatic challenge @hyp:selective-energy. The PFC is the region most sensitive to perfusion drops because its metabolic rate is highest. Even modest CBF reduction during upright posture preferentially starves the PFC, producing postural cognitive impairment — "brain fog that worsens when standing" — as a predictable hemodynamic consequence of PFC metabolic vulnerability.

*Line 2 — Brain glucose hypometabolism.* FDG-PET imaging documents regional brain glucose hypometabolism in ME/CFS @siessmeier2003pet @VanDerGucht2017MMFFDG. PFC-specific glucose metabolism has not been isolated in ME/CFS studies, but Zametkin et al. (1990) showed 8.1% lower global cerebral glucose metabolism in ADHD adults, with the largest reductions in superior prefrontal cortex @Zametkin1990. The ADHD brain is a PFC-hypometabolic brain. If ME/CFS produces cerebral hypometabolism, the affected regions should overlap with those identified in ADHD — and the existing ME/CFS FDG-PET data, though not PFC-specific, are consistent with this prediction.

*Line 3 — Cerebral creatine depletion.* Godlewska et al. (2024) documented brain creatine deficiency in ME/CFS by 7T MRS, with significant depletion in dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (+2.9% recovery after supplementation) and pregenual anterior cingulate (+8.3%) @Godlewska2024creatineMRS. The PCr shuttle is the brain's rapid ATP buffer — essential for the high burst demands of working memory and executive function. PFC creatine depletion means the PFC cannot buffer ATP demand spikes, directly predicting: impaired sustained attention (the PFC runs out of ATP mid-task), impaired working memory (insufficient PCr for persistent firing), and impaired impulse inhibition (the ATP-expensive inhibitory networks fail first).

*Line 4 — Dopaminergic-noradrenergic deficit.* The Walitt et al. (2024) NIH deep phenotyping study documented reduced CSF catecholamines in ME/CFS @walitt2024deep. PFC D1 dopamine receptors and α2A noradrenergic receptors operate on an inverted-U dose-response curve: too little catecholamine tone impairs executive function via insufficient receptor activation, while too much impairs it via noise amplification @Arnsten2011catecholaminePFC @Cools2011invertedU. The ME/CFS pattern — in an uncontrolled survey, 77.1% of stimulant-taking respondents reported brain-fog improvement (a combined ME/CFS + long-COVID cohort; @Eckey2025PatientReported) — is consistent with left-arm (suboptimal) PFC catecholamine tone, where low ATP availability compounds low transmitter availability to doubly impair executive circuits. The ADHD-ME/CFS pharmacological convergence — both conditions respond to methylphenidate, atomoxetine, and guanfacine — is consistent with a shared substrate, but a shared drug response does not by itself identify the shared circuit. A refinement limits what the stimulant signal means: large-sample human evidence localizes stimulant action to arousal and reward-salience networks, not attention-control networks @Kay2025stimulantarousalreward, so stimulant benefit in ME/CFS is better read as raising arousal and effort-reward salience than as restoring executive-processing capacity — the drug makes effort feel more worthwhile while the energy deficit that limits the PFC is untouched (see @spec:stimulant-arousal-reward-not-attention; @oq:stimulant-arousal-vs-cognitive-control).

*Line 5 — Epidemiological gradient.* Childhood ADHD is present in 29.7% of adult CFS patients @SaezFrancas2012adhdcfs. ADHD traits at age 9 predict 2× chronic fatigue risk at age 18, mediated by IL-6 @Quadt2024neurodivergentfatigue. This is not merely comorbidity — it is a dose-response gradient consistent with a shared biological substrate: patients starting with lower PFC metabolic reserve (ADHD) are the first to cross the ME/CFS clinical threshold when an additional metabolic hit (infection, inflammation, trauma) further reduces CNS energy availability.

#synthesis[
*The unification.* All five lines converge on a single geometric fact: the PFC is the brain region with the highest ATP demand per gram of tissue. This means PFC function is the first to degrade under energy scarcity, regardless of whether the scarcity arises from reduced cerebral perfusion (Line 1), impaired glucose metabolism (Line 2), depleted creatine buffering (Line 3), inadequate catecholamine tone (Line 4), or inherited lower metabolic baseline (Line 5). The behavioural counterpart of that constraint is a load-dependent inattention largely mediated by working-memory impairment @Orban2018InattentiveBehaviorWM and a movement pattern that scales with executive demand @Sarver2015HyperactivityCompensatory @Dekkers2021CognitiveDemandActivity — the outside view of an executive system recruiting energy it may not have (see @spec:adhd-movement-arousal-energy). Each line is an independent path to the same endpoint — PFC energy failure — and a given patient may have one, several, or all of these pathologies superimposed. This framework proposes that the ADHD-like executive dysfunction observed in ME/CFS is plausibly not a separate comorbidity requiring a separate diagnosis, but a predictable consequence of any condition that reduces the brain's ATP budget below the Tier 5 threshold — though the stimulant evidence (Line 4) constrains this: a positive stimulant response indexes arousal/reward salience rather than restored executive capacity, so it cannot itself confirm the PFC-energy account. The question is not "does this patient have ADHD?" but "why would a brain with inadequate energy supply to the PFC produce anything other than ADHD-like executive dysfunction?"

*Clinical implication.* This framework predicts that executive function severity should correlate with objective measures of cerebral energy status — MRS PCr:ATP ratio, FDG-PET PFC glucose uptake, and CBF during orthostatic challenge — and that treatments improving cerebral energy delivery (ketogenic therapy, creatine supplementation, cerebral vasodilators) should improve executive function in proportion to the degree of baseline PFC energy deficit. It also predicts that the subjective stimulant benefit reported in the survey (77.1% brain-fog improvement among stimulant-taking respondents @Eckey2025PatientReported) is unlikely to track objective PFC metabolic abnormality directly, because the drug acts on arousal/reward salience rather than on executive capacity @Kay2025stimulantarousalreward — so stimulant response should be treated as a subjective arousal/reward signal, not as a PFC-energy probe.
] <syn:prefrontal-energy-adhd-unification>

#speculation(title: [Catecholamine vs Non-Dopaminergic Mechanisms: Attention Selection vs Processing Speed])[
A double-blind, placebo-controlled, counterbalanced three-way crossover trial in 21 adolescents with ADHD compared a high-dose L-theanine-caffeine combination against methylphenidate and placebo on a visual selective-attention task @Nawarathna2026TheanineCaffeine. Both the combination and methylphenidate reduced false alarms (target–distractor discrimination errors, P = 0.038 and P = 0.035) and increased P3b event-related-potential amplitude while decreasing P3b latency (P < 0.05) — a shared enhancement of attentional resource allocation and of the *neural speed* of target evaluation. However, only methylphenidate improved behavioral reaction time (43.89 ms, P = 0.018), while the L-theanine-caffeine combination did not. L-theanine-caffeine engages adenosine (A1/A2A antagonism by caffeine) and glutamatergic/GABAergic (theanine) mechanisms, whereas methylphenidate blocks the dopamine transporter. The pattern is not a clean selection-vs-speed dissociation: P3b latency is itself a processing-speed index, and the non-dopaminergic combination *improved* it even though it did not shorten behavioral reaction time. So the divergence is specifically between two *speed* measures — a neural speed index (P3b latency) improved in both conditions, while behavioral reaction time improved only with methylphenidate. This complicates any claim that only the dopaminergic route governs speed. The result is further qualified by the fact that the *same* trial's components act through overlapping systems: functional imaging shows caffeine and methylphenidate both enhance memory through a shared fronto-parietal default-mode network decoupling @Becker2022StimulantCognition, and L-theanine alone improved choice reaction time (SMD 0.51) in a 31-RCT meta-analysis of healthy adults @Gerolymos2026TheanineMeta — a non-dopaminergic behavioral speed effect. The present finding therefore rests on a single small trial from one research group (n=21, not independently replicated) @Nawarathna2026TheanineCaffeine @Kahathuduwa2020TheanineCaffeineADHD and does not establish that attention selection and processing speed are governed by separable neurochemical routes; it connects to the separate speculation that elevated A2A receptor density underlies ME/CFS's anomalous caffeine responses @spec:ch15-caffeine-paradox. *Falsifiability:* a pre-registered, adequately powered ADHD crossover (n ≈ 60+) demonstrating that an L-theanine-caffeine combination also shortens behavioral reaction time would confirm that the RT null in this trial was an artifact of underpowering rather than a true route difference; if instead a larger trial replicates the RT null while again improving P3b latency, it would support a dissociation between motor/premotor speed (dopamine-sensitive) and perceptual speed (not exclusively dopamine-sensitive). Three further nulls bound the claim: L-theanine is null for fatigue across 31 RCTs @Gerolymos2026TheanineMeta; caffeine shows no association with ME/CFS (only an inverse association with *severe fatigue*, not the syndrome) @Palacios2023MECFSRiskFactors; and caffeine monotherapy shows no ADHD benefit — the effect is combination-dependent @AlShahab2025ADHDSupplements. The hypothesis that adenosine-receptor antagonism "masks rather than restores" the underlying deficit is equally consistent with the data, so the adenosine route's causal role in ME/CFS is *currently unsupported and probably null* — the default assumption is no causal role until a direct test shows otherwise. *Consequence:* in ME/CFS, where PFC catecholamine tone is suboptimal (77.1% report stimulant benefit @Eckey2025PatientReported), this trial cautions against assuming that a nutraceutical that improves one attention measure will improve them all — P3b latency improved while behavioral reaction time did not — but L-theanine is null for fatigue @Gerolymos2026TheanineMeta, caffeine carries withdrawal-fatigue and tolerance harm @Carbone2025CaffeineAging and no ME/CFS association @Palacios2023MECFSRiskFactors, and the entire edifice rests on a single small trial from one research group (n=21, acute single-dose, ADHD cohort, no ME/CFS data, not independently replicated) @Nawarathna2026TheanineCaffeine @Kahathuduwa2020TheanineCaffeineADHD. It therefore remains cross-disease mechanism context, NOT an ME/CFS treatment. (Raw certainty 0.55, ADHD disease-model population → discounted to 0.41; severity applicability unknown — study cohort not stratified by severity; evidence: general-population/ADHD crossover RCT, not ME/CFS-specific; origin: literature integration.) 
] <spec:theanine-caffeine-attention-differential>

#open-question(title: [Does ME/CFS Show Preserved Accuracy With Selective Speed Loss, or a General Accuracy Deficit?])[
The L-theanine-caffeine trial offers a natural experiment for the ME/CFS PFC-energy question, but it does not yield a single clean prediction. On this task, *errors* index target-distractor discrimination while *reaction time* indexes speed of deployment. One reading — that the non-dopaminergic (adenosine/glutamatergic) selection route is comparatively spared while the dopaminergic speed route fails — would predict a "slow-but-accurate" ME/CFS phenotype: preserved error rates with selectively slowed reaction time @Nawarathna2026TheanineCaffeine. But the section's own energy-triage framework points the other way: because the PFC's ATP-expensive *inhibitory* networks fail first under energy scarcity (Tier 5), a selective-attention task might instead produce *increased* false alarms (a commission-error/accuracy deficit) alongside slowed responses — a "slow-and-inaccurate" phenotype, the opposite dissociation. Which pattern ME/CFS patients show is an open empirical question, and both outcomes are informative: preserved accuracy with selective speed loss would support a comparatively spared selection route; an accuracy deficit would support the framework's prediction that inhibition fails before discrimination. *Falsifiability:* an objective selective-attention task in ME/CFS (vs. matched healthy and depressed controls) that shows proportionally impaired errors and reaction time, or normal reaction time, falsifies the "slow-but-accurate" reading; preserved accuracy with selectively slowed reaction time supports it; an accuracy deficit with slowed responses supports the impulse-inhibition-first reading. The three outcomes are mutually exclusive and each is testable. *Consequence:* whichever pattern replicates, it gives clinicians and researchers an objective cognitive-behavioral readout of the PFC energy/catecholamine state that is cheaper than imaging or EEG, and it sharpens the distinction between ME/CFS brain fog and the uniformly-slowed cognition of primary depression — but both candidate phenotypes are currently predictions from one ADHD crossover trial, not established ME/CFS findings. (Certainty: 0.20; evidence: cross-disease extrapolation from a single ADHD trial — mechanistic, not ME/CFS-specific; severity applicability unknown; origin: brainstorm.)
] <oq:slow-but-accurate-mecfs-signature>

=== Movement-Linked Arousal Recruitment: The Behavioural Counterpart

The lines above describe the *supply* side of prefrontal energy failure. A parallel body of ADHD research describes the *behavioural recruitment* side of the same executive system. Gross motor activity rises with central-executive demand — under every working-memory (WM) condition relative to a low-demand control, and more so in ADHD @Dekkers2021CognitiveDemandActivity @Rapport2009HyperactivityWMDeficits — and, within children with ADHD, higher activity is associated with *better* (though not normalised) WM performance, an association that reverses in typically developing children @Sarver2015HyperactivityCompensatory. Activity stays elevated while central-executive load is sustained and falls once the task becomes predictable @Kofler2015ActivityWM. Attentional behaviour follows the same load gradient: intact during a low-WM-demand activity, markedly worse during classroom instruction, with 59% of the inattention gap statistically mediated by working-memory impairment @Orban2018InattentiveBehaviorWM. Stimulant medication and endogenous movement appear to act on a shared arousal substrate @Wiegers2024StimulantActivityWM.

Read against the PFC-energy framework developed above, this suggests that the executive system normally *recruits* arousal/energy to sustain persistent firing, and that ME/CFS may be the supply-side counterpart in which the budget itself is constrained, so that recruitment (movement, effort, or stimulant) cannot restore capacity — consistent with the interpretation of stimulant benefit as an arousal/effort-reward signal rather than restored capacity @Kay2025stimulantarousalreward @spec:stimulant-arousal-reward-not-attention. The recruitment reading is not the only account: delay aversion frames the same movement as escape from aversive delay @SonugaBarke2003DualPathway, a state-regulation deficit frames it as failure to hold an optimal activation state @Metin2016StateRegulationEventRate @Drescher2021PupillometryStateRegulation, and stimulation is not uniformly helpful @Abikoff1996AuditoryStimulation @Antrop2005WaitingActivity. The cross-disease bridge is developed in full at @spec:adhd-movement-arousal-energy (ch14d); it is a cross-disease inference with no ME/CFS-cohort data.

#speculation(title: [Arousal as a Second Input to the Executive Energy Budget])[
*Certainty: 0.30. Origin: brainstorm.* This section models executive function as a threshold function of the ATP budget: below the Tier 5 threshold, working memory and inhibition fail. The ADHD movement data suggest the model is missing a second input. Gross motor activity rises to a WM condition and then stays relatively flat rather than scaling with load @Dekkers2021CognitiveDemandActivity; activity tracks *sustained* (unpredictable) central-executive load and falls when the load becomes predictable @Kofler2015ActivityWM; and within children with ADHD more movement predicts better WM while the same movement predicts worse WM in typically developing children @Sarver2015HyperactivityCompensatory. A single ATP-threshold model predicts monotone load-dependence, not a step-up that persists. A two-input model — an ATP budget gated by a recruitable arousal signal — fits better: the executive system must both *have* energy and *allocate* it, and allocation (arousal) may fail independently of the budget. This reframes the ME/CFS question from "how much energy is left" to "how much energy is left *and* can it be recruited to the PFC", which is the same distinction the stimulant evidence draws between arousal/effort-reward salience and restored capacity @Kay2025stimulantarousalreward @spec:stimulant-arousal-reward-not-attention.

*Falsifiable prediction:* a two-input model (ATP budget × arousal recruitment) will predict the observed step-up activity pattern and the medication×activity interaction @Wiegers2024StimulantActivityWM better than a single ATP threshold; in ME/CFS, PFC function will track the *product* of an energetic index (MRS PCr:ATP, creatine) and an arousal index, so that patients with adequate energy but low arousal, and those with low energy but intact arousal, show distinguishable cognitive profiles. Falsified if PFC function tracks a single energetic index regardless of arousal state, or if the step-up pattern is fully explained by a one-input model.

*Limitations:* The two-input structure is a modelling proposal, not a fitted model; no study has measured ATP budget and arousal simultaneously with executive function in the same cohort. The arousal construct is itself contested (see competing mechanisms below). Cross-disease inference: ADHD cohorts → ME/CFS, no ME/CFS data. *Severity applicability: unknown.* *Consequence:* it makes the energy-triage model *falsifiable in two dimensions instead of one* — a patient could fail executive function because the budget is low, because recruitment is impaired, or both — which changes both the mechanistic target and what a treatment is expected to do.
] <spec:arousal-recruitment-executive-budget>

#speculation(title: [Movement Urge as a Cognitive-Load Signal for Pacing])[
*Certainty: 0.30. Origin: brainstorm.* If spontaneous movement indexes executive load in ADHD @Rapport2009HyperactivityWMDeficits @Dekkers2021CognitiveDemandActivity, then the *urge to move or fidget* during cognitive work may be a bodily read-out of load that precedes the subjective sense of cognitive fatigue. For a pacing strategy this is attractive because it would be zero-cost and pre-emptive. The proposal is deliberately narrow: it uses movement urge as a *signal*, not as a therapy — it does not claim that moving improves cognition in ME/CFS, and it must not be read as encouraging activity in patients who are PEM-prone.

*Falsifiable prediction:* in ME/CFS patients, self-reported or actigraphically detected restlessness during a cognitive task will predict subsequent cognitive-performance deterioration (within-session and next-day) better than subjective fatigue rating does, and earlier. Falsified if restlessness does not precede or predict cognitive deterioration, or if it is simply proportional to task duration rather than to load.

*Limitations:* The inference is one step removed from the source data — the ADHD studies link movement to *concurrent* performance, not to a *predictive* load signal, and none measured a subsequent crash. Restlessness in ME/CFS could equally reflect autonomic/orthostatic discomfort rather than executive load. Cross-disease inference with no ME/CFS data. *Severity applicability: unknown — most plausibly relevant to mild/moderate patients who perform cognitive work, but untested.* *Consequence:* if it holds, patients gain an early, pre-symptomatic cue to stop cognitive work before PEM, rather than discovering the limit after the crash.
] <spec:movement-urge-pacing-signal>
